      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 European Dialysis and Transplant Nurses Association/European Renal Care Association.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Current protocols recommend the use of a blunt needle to access the arteriovenous fistula via a buttonhole. This study aims to demonstrate whether a sharp needle can be used at the same buttonhole site without causing complications."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "GOAL",
            "content": "To measure and compare fistula cannulation failures between the use of blunt and sharp needles. PLAN: Open-crossover randomised controlled trial."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Adult out-patients who had provided consent and were on dialysis with a mature arteriovenous fistula and buttonhole cannulation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Failed cannulation-difficulty in inserting the needle and the trampoline effect; incidence of complications, such as infection and haematomas; times to haemostasis; patients' pain; and patients' preferences."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Based on analysis of the data from the 35 patients enrolled, no significant differences were detected in failed cannulation of the fistulae between the use of a blunt needle and a sharp needle for the 335 venous accesses (p\u2009=\u20090.071). However, a significant difference was detected for the 335 arterial accesses (p\u2009=\u20090.001), in which the sharp needle was more effective. Significant differences were also detected in the difficulty of insertion and in the trampoline effect for both venous and arterial access (p\u2009<\u20090.05); the use of a sharp needle was more favourable."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "This study demonstrates an increased incidence of failed cannulation using a blunt needle compared with using a sharp needle, although this was not significant. In addition, the use of a sharp needle did not result in any increase in complications."
          }
        ]
      },
